    "body": "Westport Fuel Systems Inc. changed its name to Aetherion Technologies Inc. on Thursday. The Vancouver, British Columbia, company said its next push is bringing Cespira’s high-pressure direct injection (HPDI) fuel system to North American long-haul fleets on compressed natural gas .\nThe rename follows Westport’s July 2025 sale of its light-duty business to an investment vehicle of Netherlands-based Heliaca Investments for a base price of $79.5 million. A company brand sheet ties the new name to that divestiture and to the transfer of the Westport name.\nAetherion’s chief executive said the rename tells the market who the company is after a strategy reset that narrowed what it works on.\n“The bread and butter is the original foundation of the company, the original technology, HPDI, high pressure direct injection,” said Dan Sceli, CEO of Aetherion, in an interview with FreightWaves. “That’s really how it all started and that’s the core that we want to get back to focusing on and not trying to do 20 things part way, but do a couple of things really well.”\nThe name draws on two Greek roots, according to the company: aithēr, the clean air of the upper sky, and ion, meaning motion or energy.\nShareholders approved a name change at the company’s June 30 annual and special meeting. The company expects its shares to trade under the new name on the Toronto Stock Exchange and Nasdaq, subject to final exchange approvals, on a date it has not announced.\nHPDI anchors the renamed company\nPhilip Hill co-founded Westport in 1995 and invented HPDI, which keeps the diesel engine and changes the fuel it burns. HPDI is sold through Cespira, a joint venture with Volvo Group launched in 2024. Aetherion owns 55% and Volvo owns 45%.\nHPDI runs in more than 12,000 trucks worldwide, according to the company. Cespira posted second-quarter revenue of $27.1 million, up 125% from $12 million a year earlier, according to Westport’s second-quarter management’s discussion and analysis , dated Aug. 11.\nThe open question now, he said, is how quickly fleets switch to natural gas for its stable pricing.\n“There’s lots of places for battery electric and fuel cells, but not on these long-haul truck fleets,” he said.\nHPDI can also run hydrogen and natural gas blends on the same core technology, Sceli said. Most of hydrogen’s cost is tied up in liquefying and transporting it, he said, and it needs to get closer to $5 an equivalent gallon to compete. He sees blending hydrogen into existing U.S. natural gas pipelines as one way around that cost.\n“They’ve been doing it in Hawaii for 50 years,” he said.\nHawaiʻi Gas blends up to 15% hydrogen into the synthetic natural gas it makes at a plant built in 1974 and distributes by pipeline on Oʻahu.\nCespira and Volvo signed a development agreement in June to run Volvo’s 13-liter engine on hydrogen through HPDI, with a European commercial launch targeted before 2030, according to Westport’s second-quarter filing.\nCNG storage for North America\nCespira’s growth so far has come on liquefied natural gas, Sceli said. North America is the next market, he said, and its fuel is compressed natural gas, with pipelines and infrastructure already in place.\nWestport showed a CNG demonstration truck at ACT Expo in May, a Volvo VNL 300 on a high-pressure storage system. The company reported more than 600 miles of range and up to 10 miles per gallon equivalent on that truck. Sceli said a number of large fleets have shown interest, and Volvo is working through its business case for the system in North America. He told FreightWaves in April that Environmental Protection Agency certification is the primary step toward commercialization.\nFleets want price stability on top of savings, Sceli said.\n“They want to know that six months or 12 months down the road, what their fuel pricing is going to be,” he said. “This volatility in diesel causes headaches for all of these fleets and pricing and dealing with short- and long-term contracts.”\nOver the next six to 12 months, Sceli said, the two things to watch are Cespira’s growth and the CNG system.\n“Westport is bringing a new CNG storage system to combine it with HPDI for the North American market, because the market is looking for an alternative and we have it,” he said.\nUpcoming FreightWaves Events\nCompliance\nBrokerage Compliance Symposium\nThe day before F3. Every compliance issue you face - fraud exposure, carrier liability, FMCSA rules, cargo theft, insurance gaps - navigated by attorneys and operators defining best practices in a changing industry.\nOctober 26, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nAwards\nF3 Awards Dinner\nThe night before F3. FreightTech100 companies honored. FreightTech 25 and Shipper of Choice winners revealed live. Cocktail reception into dinner and live music - 300 industry leaders in one purpose-built room.\nOctober 26, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nFreightTech\nF3: Future of Freight Festival\nIndustry-defining keynotes, rapid-fire technology demos, and industry leaders networking in experiences across Chattanooga - plus the inaugural F3 Awards Dinner featuring the FreightTech and Shipper of Choice reveals.\nOctober 27, 2026 – October 28, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nCompliance Brokerage Compliance Symposium Oct 26 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nThe day before F3. Every compliance issue you face - fraud exposure, carrier liability, FMCSA rules, cargo theft, insurance gaps - navigated by attorneys and operators defining best practices in a changing industry.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now\nAwards F3 Awards Dinner Oct 26 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nThe night before F3. FreightTech100 companies honored. FreightTech 25 and Shipper of Choice winners revealed live. Cocktail reception into dinner and